.TH FB 3
.SH NAME
fb, fbctl \- the screen as a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #c /dev
.nf

.B /dev/fb
.B /dev/fbctl
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Fb
is the screen's own memory, addressed as a file.
A byte at offset
.RI y " * pitch + " x " * bytes-per-pixel"
is the low byte of the pixel at column
.I x
and row
.IR y ,
and a write there changes it.
.B Fbctl
beside it reports the geometry a program needs to do that arithmetic, for
example:
.IP
.EX
size 1280 800
pitch 5120
depth 32
r 8 16
g 8 8
b 8 0
.EE
.PP
One fact per line:
.B size
in pixels,
.B pitch
in bytes per row,
.B depth
in bits per pixel, and
.BR r ,
.B g
and
.B b
as each colour channel's width in bits and its shift into the pixel word.
There is nothing yet that can be set about the hardware, so every write to
.B fbctl
is refused with
.BR EINVAL .
.PP
Every other file this device serves has no length, because its contents are
the future.
.B Fb
is the exception: it has exactly
.RI height " * pitch"
bytes, all of them present at once, and a read or a write names which of
them with its offset.
A read at or past the end answers zero bytes, a true end of file.
A read or a write that straddles the end moves what fits and reports that
count.
A write that starts at or past the end is refused with
.BR ENOSPC ,
because there is no room at that offset and a count of zero would only
invite trying again at the same place.
.PP
Nothing serialises two writers to
.BR fb .
Two programs drawing the same pixels tear the picture, not any state the
kernel keeps, and the kernel's own console shares the surface on exactly
those terms.
.PP
While any fid holds
.B fb
open, the console stops drawing on the glass and draws into a copy instead,
so a program that opens
.B fb
gets the screen to itself.
The console keeps working at its own pace, and nothing is lost.
When the last such fid closes, the whole frame -- not only the region the
console used -- is copied back, so anything the holder painted stays on
the screen until something draws over it.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/devfs/fbdev.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR cons (3),
.IR draw (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A write to
.B fbctl
of any bytes at all fails with
.BR EINVAL .
A write to
.B fb
starting at or past the end of the frame fails with
.BR ENOSPC .
